## 2026-09-05 targeted addition — `exportFilename` (plan AC-A6, Task 2)

The plan was amended after this suite's first authoring pass to add Task 2
(`exportFilename(header, kind)`), extracting `src/app/api.ts`'s previously
untested `praesto-export.<ext>` fallback literal into the same pure module.
This closes exactly the coverage gap `test-reviewer` had flagged: neither the
parser's own suite (correctly scoped to `parseFilenameFromContentDisposition`'s
`null`-vs-not behavior) nor Task 8's manual device pass (both real routes
always send a header, per AC-6) ever exercised the fallback string itself.

No existing test in `test/content-disposition.test.ts` was touched — only the
import statement gained `exportFilename` alongside the existing
`parseFilenameFromContentDisposition` import, and 7 new cases were appended in
2 new `describe` blocks:

- `exportFilename` — parsed name takes priority over the fallback (plan AC-A6):
  2 cases (JSON and `.ics`) proving the parsed name wins over the fallback
  when the header yields one — guards against an implementation that always
  returns the fallback regardless of the parse result.
- `exportFilename` — undated fallback on a missing or unparseable header
  (plan AC-A6): 5 cases — exact-string fallback for `null` header (both
  kinds), for a header with no filename parameter, for an unrelated-parameter
  header, and a same-inputs-different-kind case pinning that the extension
  tracks `kind` rather than both downloads colliding on one literal. Every
  fallback assertion is an exact-string `.toBe(...)`, never a loose pattern,
  per the amendment's own stated design: the fallback is deliberately undated
  so a server-side header regression is visually obvious rather than blending
  in as a plausible dated name.

Outcome: `NEW_TEST_REQUIRED` for AC-A6, additive-only inside the existing file
(see updated AC outcomes table above).

